<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lagny-sur-Marne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lagny-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> is a town on the Marne east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which grew around the abbey of Saint-Pierre. In the 12th and 13th centuries it hosted one of the Champagne fairs, which opened the annual cycle each January and drew merchants from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It now lies in the Seine-et-Marne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> within the Paris region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lagny-sur-marne.jpg" alt="Lagny-sur-Marne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chabe01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%B4tel_ville_Lagny_Marne_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 644 Lagny-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> was the site of Lagny Abbey, a monastery founded that year. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> destroyed it, it was refounded about 990. The state seized the monastery at the French Revolution, and since 1842 its buildings have housed the offices of the municipality. The Hôtel de Ville, established in the buildings of the former abbey, was completed in 1765. The town was also one of the hosts of the Champagne fairs in the 12th and 13th centuries. In 1846 Lagny annexed the commune of Saint-Denis-du-Port.</p><p>During the Middle Ages Lagny was one of the most popular places for tourneys in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. A notable tournament was held there in November 1179 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> in honour of the coronation of his son. In 1170 the young knight Baldwin of Bethune and his lifelong friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a> had been at the court of Henry the Young King, and at the great international tournament of Lagny in 1180 Baldwin was a knight banneret leading the Flemish team, while William headed the English team.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The commune lies in the Seine-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>, 26.1 km from the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, about 25 minutes by train from the Gare de l'Est. It was officially called simply Lagny until 1971, and is part of the Val de Bussy sector of the new town of Marne-la-Vallée. Its inhabitants are called Latignaciens or Laniaques. The railway station, Lagny–Thorigny, stands across the Marne from the town centre in the neighbouring commune of Thorigny-sur-Marne. Lagny has been twinned with Sainte-Agathe-des-Monts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> since 1969, and also with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alnwick">Alnwick</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lagny-sur-Marne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts protected the cycle of fairs at Troyes, Provins, <b>Lagny</b> and Bar-sur-Aube, which made Champagne the commercial crossroads of 12th- and 13th-century Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-champagne">County of Champagne</a></li></ul></section>
